e¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡And when a convenient day was come, that Herod on his birthday made a supper to his lords, high captains, and chief estates of Galilee;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÅand when the daughter of the said Herodias came in, and danced, and pleased Herod and them that sat with him, the king said unto the damsel, Ask of me whatsoever thou wilt, and I will give it thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiAnd he sware unto her, Whatsoever thou shalt ask of me, I will give it thee, unto the half of my kingdom.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkAnd she went forth, and said unto her mother, What shall I ask? And she said, The head of John the Baptist.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ–And she came in straightway with haste unto the king, and asked, saying, I will that thou give me by and by in a charger the head of John the Baptist.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{And the king was exceeding sorry; yet for his oath's sake, and for their sakes which sat with him, he would not reject her.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd immediately the king sent an executioner, and commanded his head to be brought: and he went and beheaded him in the prison,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcand brought his head in a charger, and gave it to the damsel: and the damsel gave it to her mother.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\And when his disciples heard of it, they came and took up his corpse, and laid it in a tomb.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book(§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Jesus, and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§And he said unto them, Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while: for there were many coming and going, and they had no leisure so much as to eat.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8And they departed into a desert place by ship privately.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book(§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse,¦versesœ„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒAnd the people saw them departing, and many knew him, and ran afoot thither out of all cities, and outwent them, and came together unto him.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ²And Jesus, when he came out, saw much people, and was moved with compassion toward them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd: and he began to teach them many things.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚And when the day was now far spent, his disciples came unto him, and said, This is a desert place, and now the time is far passed:¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsend them away, that they may go into the country round about, and into the villages, and buy themselves bread: for they have nothing to eat.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ–He answered and said unto them, Give ye them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙlHe saith unto them, How many loaves have ye? go and see. And when they knew, they say, Five, and two fishes.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMAnd he commanded them to make all sit down by companies upon the green grass.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8And they sat down in ranks, by hundreds, and by fifties.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÕAnd when he had taken the five loaves and the two fishes, he looked up to heaven, and blessed, and brake the loaves, and gave them to his disciples to set before them; and the two fishes divided he